<article class="env-remark" id="0DB0">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0DB0">Remark <span data-tag="0DB0">25.12.7</span>.</a> Let $\mathcal{C}$ be a site. Let $K$ and $L$ be objects of $\text{SR}(\mathcal{C})$. Write $K = \{ U_ i\} _{i \in I}$ and $L = \{ V_ j\} _{j \in J}$. Assume $U = \coprod _{i \in I} U_ i$ and $V = \coprod _{j \in J} V_ j$ exist. Then we get </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \mathop{\mathrm{Mor}}\nolimits _{\text{SR}(\mathcal{C})}(K, L) \longrightarrow \mathop{\mathrm{Mor}}\nolimits _\mathcal {C}(U, V)  \]
</div>
  <p> as follows. Given $f : K \to L$ given by $\alpha : I \to J$ and $f_ i : U_ i \to V_{\alpha (i)}$ we obtain a transformation of functors </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \mathop{\mathrm{Mor}}\nolimits _\mathcal {C}(V, -) = \prod \nolimits _{j \in J} \mathop{\mathrm{Mor}}\nolimits _\mathcal {C}(V_ j, -) \to \prod \nolimits _{i \in I} \mathop{\mathrm{Mor}}\nolimits _\mathcal {C}(U_ i, -) = \mathop{\mathrm{Mor}}\nolimits _\mathcal {C}(U, -)  \]
</div>
  <p> sending $(g_ j)_{j \in J}$ to $(g_{\alpha (i)} \circ f_ i)_{i \in I}$. Hence the Yoneda lemma produces the corresponding map $U \to V$. Of course, $U \to V$ maps the summand $U_ i$ into the summand $V_{\alpha (i)}$ via the morphism $f_ i$. </p>
</article>